Atomistic Investigation of Grain Boundary Fracture in Alumina
Jingyuan Yan, Shun Kondo,* Bin Feng, Naoya Shibata, and Yuichi Ikuhara*
Cite This: Nano Lett. 2024, 24, 3112−3117 Read Online
ACCESS Metrics & More Article Recommendations s*ı Supporting Information
ABSTRACT: Grain boundary (GB) fracture is a major mechanism of material failure in polycrystalline ceramics. However, the intricate atomic arrangements of GBs have impeded our understanding of the atomistic mechanisms of these processes. In this study, we investigated the atomic-scale crack propagation behavior of an α-Al2O3 ∑13 grain boundary, using a combination of in situ transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and scanning TEM. The atomic-scale fracture path along the GB core was directly determined by the observation of the atomic structures of the fractured surfaces, which is consistent with density functional theory calculations. We found that the GB fracture can be attributed to the weaker local bonds and a smaller number of bonds along the fracture path. Our findings provide atomistic insights into the mechanisms of crack propagation along GBs, offering significant implications for GB engineering and the toughening of ceramics.
KEYWORDS: grain boundary, fracture, α-Al2O3, atomistic mechanism, in situ indentation
B
rittle fracture leads to disastrous damage to materials and thus has been an important research topic for many
decades.1−3 In practical materials that are often used in polycrystalline forms, grain boundaries (GBs) often act as the
preferential crack propagation paths.4−6 Therefore, understanding the underlying mechanism of GB fracture (or intergranular fracture) is crucial for controlling the brittleness of materials and developing tougher materials. The basic theory to illustrate the fracture phenomena was established in 1921, when Griffith, for the first time, described brittle fracture as a surface nucleation process under thermodynamic
equilibrium.7 According to his theory, the mechanical stability of a crack can be described as a balance between the crack driving force, represented by energy release rate G, and surface energy γs of the two fracture surfaces, with the relationship G = 2γs. Later, the importance of atomistic studies of fracture beyond the continuum approximation was recognized, and the fracture process was further interpreted as the sequential bond
breaking processes.8−10 More recently, computational methods such as density functional theory (DFT) and molecular dynamics (MD) calculations are used to simulate the atomistic
fracture processes and to predict the fracture toughness.11−13 However, the cracking processes at the atomic scale remain
controversial due to the lack of the experimental evidence.14 Especially for fracture along GBs, the complicated atomic arrangement along the GB core makes it difficult to reveal the atomic-scale mechanism of cracking. The lack of a fundamental understanding of GB fracture has impeded us from making
further progress in material design and fabrication.15,16 Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) is a powerful tool for the direct observation of the crack propagation processes as well as atomic-scale structures of the initial and cracked
GBs.17−19 Specifically, the well-developed in situ TEM nanoindentation techniques can exert local stresses at a certain GB by precisely controlling the movement of a sharp indenter tip inside a TEM, making the crack nucleate and propagate along specific GBs. The atomic structures of the fractured surfaces can be directly characterized using aberrationcorrected scanning TEM (STEM). In this study, we studied the atomic-scale fracture processes of an α-Al2O3 GB via in situ TEM nanoindentation, STEM imaging, and first-principles calculations. α-Al2O3 is one of the most widely used structural ceramics that exhibits intergranular fracture, and toughening of the Al2O3 GB has been an
important research topic for decades.20−22 We selected the ∑13 GB as a model GB, which has an interfacial structure that is very different from the bulk atomic environment and can be
representative for general GBs.23,24 Here we fabricated the ∑13 GB by a bicrystal method and introduced a nanoscale crack along the GB through in situ nanoindentation inside a TEM instrument. We then characterized the atomic structures of the fractured surfaces from two orthogonal directions. Via combination with DFT calculations, the atomic-scale fracture paths of this GB were revealed. The atomic structure of the ∑13 GB was first characterized prior to the nanoindentation experiment. We observed the atomic structure of the ∑13 GB from two orthogonal
