appeared inside the bulk region. Afterward, a crack was nucleated at the point at which the indenter tip was contacted, and the introduced crack slowly propagated along the GB in a controlled manner. We stopped the insertion of the tip when the crack length was ∼200 nm. In this way, we created fresh crack surfaces without additional sample preparation that may introduce artifacts into the specimen; e.g., thinning of the specimen is required if indentation is performed on a bulk specimen. After the tip was retracted, the two fracture surfaces moved close to each other because of the geometry of the original sample. As a result, the stress was concentrated at the crack tip, with some contours observed in the TEM image. However, as shown in the low-magnification HAADF-STEM image in Figure 1d, a nanoscale gap between the surfaces is observed. Furthermore, the contrast in the bulk region around the surfaces is rather uniform, indicating that there is no severe sample bending. Thus, characterization of the atomic structures of the fractured surface is feasible. On the contrary, the two grains at the crack tip area show different contrasts, indicating a misorientation caused by the stress concentration. A similar experiment was also performed from the [2̅021] direction of this GB. To determine the atomic-scale fracture path, the fractured surfaces of the GB were characterized from the two orthogonal directions and compared with those of the intact GB structures. STEM characterizations were conducted on each side of the two fractured surfaces. As shown in panels a and c of Figure 2, all surfaces are flat and parallel to the (101̅4) plane, which is also the GB plane. The Al contrast at each surface drastically decreases in the outermost atomic layer, showing a similar characteristic of the contrast change at the intact GB. Via combination of corresponding ABF-STEM images, the Al atoms in the outmost layer could be marked as blue dots (shown in Figure 2b,d). We found that on both fracture surfaces, the surface Al atoms show continuous periodicity with the Al sites in the bulk. Simply putting the two surfaces together would yield the intact GB. All of these features strongly suggest the crack propagated along the center O layer. To verify the experimentally determined GB fracture path, DFT calculations were performed. The external energy required to form a crack could be evaluated as the difference in energy between the two new fractured surfaces and the
original intact GB.27 For ionic crystals, a stable fractured surface should have a charge neutral surface layer or no dipole moment perpendicular to the surface. In the ∑13 GB, the stacking sequence of the atomic layer along the [505̅4] direction is preserved as −O1−O2−O3−Al1−Al2−, and fracture paths within the O2 layer or between Al1 and Al2 are the possible path for producing a charge neutral (101̅4) surface. Thus, we calculated the cleavage energy of all such possible fracture paths, as denoted in Figure 3. Paths 1 and 2 describe fracture paths within the center O layer and between Al−Al layers at the GB core, respectively. For comparison, we also considered the possible fracture paths next to the GB core (paths 3 and 4) and in the bulk part (paths 5 and 6). The calculated Ecleavage values are summarized in Table 1. One can see that GB O cleavage
(path 1) exhibits the lowest cleavage energy of 4.24 J/m2, while the neighboring Al−Al path exhibits a much higher cleavage energy. In addition, the energies of both O cleavage and Al cleavage increase as it moves away from the GB plane. These results strongly suggest that the fracture along the centered O
layer should be energetically favorable, which is in good accordance with our experimental observation. To reveal the atomistic origin governing the fracture path selection, we compared the local bonding environment along the O cleavage paths at the GB (path 1) and bulk (path 5). Figure 4a shows the local bonding environment in the GB core and the bulk region from the two orthogonal directions, and Figure 4b shows magnified figures of the corresponding structural units of both regions. Each unit contains six Al atoms (Al1−Al6) and five O atoms (a−e). In both units, Al1/Al2 and Al4/Al5 are overlapped when observed from the [1̅21̅0] direction. The Al−O bonding distances along the GB O (path 1) and bulk O (path 5) are summarized in Table 2. The largest differences are found for the Al3−Oc and Al6−Oa distances (italic values in Table 2), with distances measured to be 2.89 and 3.16 Å, respectively, in the GB. These distances have far
exceeded the largest ion radius sum of Al3+ and O2− reported
previously,16,28 which can be regarded as unbonded (marked with dashed lines in Figure 4b). Therefore, fracture along the GB O plane needs to break only two Al−O bonds (Al3−Oe and Al1−Oe or Al6−Oe and Al4−Oe) around the centered Oe in the unit, while fracture along the bulk O (path 5) needs two more Al−O bonds (Al3−Oc and Al6−Oa) to be broken. This difference in the number of Al−O bonds to be broken should contribute to the lower energy for GB O cleavage. In addition, it should be noted that the bond lengths of the four Al−O bonds connected with the centered Oe also differ between the GB and the bulk units. In the bulk unit, the Al−O bonds at the bulk are either 1.88 or 1.97 Å, the same as that in the standard Al2O3 primitive cell. However, much longer bonds, 2.11 and 2.13 Å, are formed at the GB (Al3−O and Al4−O, highlighted with bold font in Table 2), while the rest of bond lengths have the same level as those in the bulk. Because
the bond strengths are directly related to the bond length,29−32
