the longer distance between the atoms would introduce weaker bonds and therefore “weak points” into the dynamic cleavage processes. These results suggest that bonding between Al and O is overall weakened at the GB core, which further validated the GB O cleavage path as the preferential fracture path. In summary, the fracture processes along the ∑13 Al2O3 GB were studied at the atomic scale with in situ TEM nanoindentation, STEM characterization, and first-principles calculation. The as-fractured surfaces were obtained by cracking the GB inside the TEM instrument, and the atomic structures of the fractured surfaces were characterized from two orthogonal directions. The experimental results suggest that the crack should propagate along the centered O layer at the GB core, which agrees with the theoretical calculation. The origin of the GB fracture is found to be related to the weaker bonds and the smaller number of bonds at the GB core, which results in a lower cleavage energy. Our findings provide new insights into the atomistic process of GB fracture and are greatly important for GB engineering in toughened ceramics.
