METHODS
Sample Preparation. In this study, a bicrystal method was
used to fabricate a well-defined model GB.33 The ∑13 Al2O3 bicrystal was fabricated by the thermal diffusion bonding of two single crystals (Shinkosha Co., Ltd.) at 1500 °C under 0.1
MPa for 10 h in air.25 The bicrystallographic orientation of this GB is summarized as (101̅4)top//(101̅4)bottom, [12̅10]top// [1̅21̅0]bottom, and [2̅021]top// [202 1]bottom. TEM samples with the well-defined GBs placed in the middle were prepared by a focused ion beam (Helios 5, FEI). Two TEM lamellas (6 μm × 8 μm × 1 μm) containing a single GB were extracted from the bulk bicrystal from the [1̅21̅0] and [2̅021] directions, with (1̅21̅0) and (2̅021) lamella planes, respectively. The lamellas were welded to the top of the copper mesh and further thinned to ∼80 nm according to the parameters suggested in the
previous report.34
In Situ TEM Nanoindentation. The GBs in the thin lamellas were fractured using in situ TEM with the Bruker PI95 nanoindenter inside a TEM instrument (JEM-2100HC, JEOL Ltd.) operated at 200 kV. The copper mesh with a thin lamella was pasted to the sample mount with silver paste, and the sample mount was further fixed in front of a diamond indenter tip in the holder. The orientation of the mesh and the sample mount were carefully adjusted to make sure the lamellas were normal to the beam direction and the GBs were parallel to the x tilt axis of the holder. The nanoindenter with a sharp diamond tip was controlled by a piezoceramic to move in three dimensions (x, y, and z). During the in situ experiment, it was controlled in a manual mode to move toward the GB, exerting a concentrated force at the GB to initiate GB fracture. The cracking process was controlled at a slow speed to realize a stable fracture, with each forwarding step of 0.1 nm when no more contrast changed in the sample. In situ indentation was performed in the two samples with [1̅21̅0] and [2̅021] orientations, producing fracture surfaces for further twodimensional STEM characterizations. The indentation movie was recorded with a frame rate of 30 frames per second by using a CCD camera for TEM (Orius SC200D, Gatan Inc.). Fracture Surface Atomic Structure Characterization. After the in situ nanoindentation experiment, the sample was removed from the sample mount and transferred to the STEM instrument for structural characterization on the atomic scale. The fracture surfaces were characterized using a JEMARM200F (JEOL Ltd.) STEM instrument equipped with an aberration corrector and a cold-field emission gun operated at 200 kV. The convergence semiangle was 23.5 mrad, and the collection semiangle was 68−280 mrad for HAADF and 12− 24 mrad for annular bright-field (ABF) imaging.
First-Principles Calculations. First-principles calculations were performed using a plane-wave-basis projector-augmented wave (PAW) method implemented in the Vienna ab initio simulation package (VASP). The generalized gradient approximation (GGA) was employed for the exchange correlation potential for Al and O. The wave functions were expanded by plane waves with a maximum kinetic energy of 500 eV. For the calculations of the ∑13 GB structure, we prepared the supercell with 200 atoms, where two equivalent GBs were included to satisfy the periodic boundary condition. All atoms were allowed to relax under a conjugate gradient
