scheme until their maximum residual force converged to 0.1 eV/nm. The cleavage energies were also determined by DFT calculations. The calculation of cleavage energies follows Griffith’s theory. In this study, we used the ideal work of
separation as the cleavage energy.27 The ideal work of separation corresponds to the energy to cleave the grain boundary in a reversible process, and a lower bound of the
external work.35 In practice, other irreversible works due to the discrete rupture of atomic bonds at the crack tip will be added
to the total work to cleave the GB.9,36,37 However, to directly calculate the actual work of the fracture, it is necessary to perform first-principles molecular dynamics simulations of the crack tip propagation along the grain boundary using a quite large calculation cell. Thus, we used the ideal work of separation here as a first approximation to compare the cleavage energies of each fracture path. The construction of the structural model of the ∑13 Al2O3 GB follows the previous
reports.23,24 Specifically, the calculated bulk GB model containing two intact GBs was cut at two equivalent fracture paths for each GB, and two slab models containing fractured surfaces were obtained. The energy of these slabs was further calculated and compared with that of the original one. The change in the total energy could be representative of the energy needed for the cleavage process, i.e., =+
